## Proof

We verify that $\iota: C \Rightarrow 1_{\text{Group}}$ is a natural transformation, where $C: \mathbf{Group} \to \mathbf{Group}$ is the commutator functor and $1_{\text{Group}}$ is the identity functor.

### Step 1. The commutator functor $C$ is well-defined

For a group $G$, set $C(G) = [G, G]$, the commutator subgroup generated by all commutators $[a, b] = aba^{-1}b^{-1}$ with $a, b \in G$.

For a group homomorphism $f: G \to H$, define $C(f): [G, G] \to [H, H]$ to be the restriction of $f$ to $[G, G]$. This is well-defined as a map into $[H, H]$ because for every generator $[a, b] \in [G, G]$,
$$
f([a, b]) = f(a)f(b)f(a)^{-1}f(b)^{-1} = [f(a), f(b)] \in [H, H],
$$
and since $f$ is a homomorphism and $[G, G]$ is generated by commutators, $f([G, G]) \subseteq [H, H]$.

$C$ is a functor:
- $C(\mathrm{id}_G) = \mathrm{id}_{[G,G]}$, since restricting the identity to $[G, G]$ gives the identity on $[G, G]$.
- For $f: G \to H$ and $g: H \to K$, $C(g \circ f) = C(g) \circ C(f)$, since both sides are the restriction of $g \circ f$ to $[G, G]$, landing in $[K, K]$.

### Step 2. The components $\iota_G$ are well-defined group homomorphisms

For each group $G$, the component
$$
\iota_G: C(G) = [G, G] \longrightarrow 1_{\text{Group}}(G) = G
$$
is the inclusion map, which is an injective group homomorphism.

### Step 3. Verification of the naturality square

Let $f: G \to H$ be any group homomorphism. We must show that the following diagram commutes:

$$
\begin{array}{ccc}
C(G) = [G, G] & \xrightarrow{\;\iota_G\;} & G \\[4pt]
{\scriptstyle C(f)}\downarrow & & \downarrow{\scriptstyle 1_{\text{Group}}(f) = f} \\[4pt]
C(H) = [H, H] & \xrightarrow{\;\iota_H\;} & H
\end{array}
$$

That is, we must verify
$$
f \circ \iota_G \;=\; \iota_H \circ C(f).
$$

Both sides are maps $[G, G] \to H$. For any $x \in [G, G]$:

- **Left-hand side:** $(f \circ \iota_G)(x) = f(\iota_G(x)) = f(x)$, since $\iota_G$ is inclusion.
- **Right-hand side:** $(\iota_H \circ C(f))(x) = \iota_H(C(f)(x)) = C(f)(x) = f(x)$, since $C(f)$ is the restriction of $f$ to $[G, G]$ and $\iota_H$ is inclusion.

Therefore
$$
(f \circ \iota_G)(x) = f(x) = (\iota_H \circ C(f))(x)
$$
for all $x \in [G, G]$, so
$$
f \circ \iota_G = \iota_H \circ C(f).
$$

### Conclusion

The naturality condition holds for every group homomorphism $f: G \to H$. Hence $\iota: C \Rightarrow 1_{\text{Group}}$ is a natural transformation. $\blacksquare$

